<section class="reference-section" id="mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Mahamaya, Madhu and Kaitabha</h2><a href="/mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Markandeya Purana · Canto 81 · Devi Mahatmya chapter 1.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the owner-supplied F. E. Pargiter translation (1904). The source PDF remains private and is not copied into the public website.</p><ol><li><strong>81.1–28 · printed pp. 465–467 · supplied PDF pp. 511–513</strong><small>The opening of the Devi Mahatmya, Suratha’s lost kingdom, Samadhi’s exile from his household and their meeting at Medhas’s hermitage.</small></li><li><strong>81.29–46 · printed pp. 468–469 · supplied PDF pp. 514–515</strong><small>Their question about attachment and Medhas’s account of Mahamaya as deluding power, supreme knowledge and cause of liberation.</small></li><li><strong>81.47–53 · printed pp. 469–470 · supplied PDF pp. 515–516</strong><small>The eternal Goddess, the primordial ocean, Vishnu’s contemplative sleep, the lotus of Brahma and the approach of Madhu and Kaitabha.</small></li><li><strong>81.54–67 · printed pp. 470–471 · supplied PDF pp. 516–517</strong><small>Brahma’s hymn to Mahamaya and his prayer that she awaken Vishnu and delude the two asuras.</small></li><li><strong>81.68–77 · printed p. 472 · supplied PDF p. 518</strong><small>Mahamaya’s withdrawal, Vishnu’s awakening, the long struggle, the offered boon and the defeat of Madhu and Kaitabha.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> The sequence and theological claims follow this supplied edition. The reflection about a changed quality of attention is editorial. The battle duration conveys the scale stated by the narrative; it is not converted into a historical chronology.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. They preserve Mahamaya’s role in awakening and delusion while keeping Vishnu as the figure who fights and defeats the asuras. Settings, faces, armour and visible attributes are artistic choices rather than historical claims.</p><a href="/devi-mahishasura.html">Continue with the middle episode</a></div></section>
